WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.080
Hi everyone, it's Jerry. This is a game from
the 2004 Reykjavik Rapid between Garry Kasparov

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:11.619
who is playing the white pieces versus Magnus
Carlsen. At the time Kasparov was rated 2831

00:00:11.619 --> 00:00:18.180
and Carlsen, still just a 13-year-old International
Master, was rated 2484. This game lasted 32

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:22.420
moves but I think it's a pretty good one to
go through because it involves arguably one

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:27.910
of the best ever chess players, Garry Kasparov,
and the number one chess player as of the

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:33.559
July 2011 Fide rating list, Magnus Carlsen.
One other interesting thing to note is that

00:00:33.559 --> 00:00:38.390
about one month after this game was played...
that's when Magnus Carlsen met all the requirements

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:42.659
for that Grandmaster title. So let's have
a look at this. We have Kasparov opening up

00:00:42.659 --> 00:00:49.550
with c4, Carlsen knight f6. Knight c3, g6
going for the King's Indian setup, and now

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:55.789
e4 putting a giant grip on that d5 square.
So there is not going to be any d5 move. If

00:00:55.789 --> 00:00:59.940
d5 did want to get played, it's going to have
to be played right now. But we don't have

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:05.860
that. g6, e4, d6, d4. So with such a pawn
center, black is certainly going to have to

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:12.040
strike at it at some point. And since d5 is
no longer on the cards, we are going to need

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:18.810
either the c-pawn or e-pawn to strike at the
white pawn center. So bishop g7, knight f3,

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:24.450
castle, bishop e2, and now e5. So black is
going with this pawn striking in the center,

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:29.590
and now bishop to e3. There is no win of a
pawn because even though this knight wins

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:34.540
the pawn in this position, it's also unprotected
and it fails... well it could just be met

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:39.550
with just knight takes pawn, discovered attack
on the knight. So instead we just have more

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:45.480
development by white. Bishop e3, developing
the bishop, watching over d4. And now black

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:52.250
gives up the center capturing on d4. Instead
we could see knight g4 attacking the bishop,

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:56.670
chasing it to g5. Just showing a few moves
deep... that's as far as I want to take this

00:01:56.670 --> 00:02:02.200
right here. f6, the bishop is hit and it has
a big decision to make. Where exactly does

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:07.480
it want to reside? The knight on the other
hand is either going to be kicked away to

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:14.190
h6, or it will voluntarily go to that h6 square
where it will then pivot on f7 and be a pretty

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:20.510
good contributor to this e5 square which is
a strong point for the black position. We

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:25.020
don't enter that territory, instead again
black gives up the center. After pawn takes,

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:29.990
how exactly to recapture? Capturing with the
queen would allow knight g4, both queen and

00:02:29.990 --> 00:02:34.280
bishop being hit... something you want to
avoid. Similarly bishop takes would allow

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:40.080
knight development with tempo. So that's why
we have knight takes, and now c6 An important

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:45.270
point is this position right here because
it may be very very tempting to just want

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:52.270
to let's say castle. And even though this
is a developing move, it could run into some

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:59.560
trouble here because after rook e8, how exactly
to meet the pressure against that e4 pawn?

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:04.550
Do you move that bishop again? Do you move
that queen to let's say c2? Let's take a look

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:09.260
at the queen c2 move. First and foremost,
does this queen really want to play the role

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:14.880
of watching over this e4 pawn? Probably not.
And that move could be met with let's say

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:19.520
just knight g4, trying to chase that bishop
away from the defense of the knight. White

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:24.630
would pretty much be forced into giving up
the bishop pair. When there's a position here

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:30.540
which is still open, I don't know if that
can really be recommended. Also if you do

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:35.660
f3, well that's now going to allow a move
like d5, taking advantage of the fact that

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:40.930
this bishop is now unprotected. And so white
could capture in this direction with that

00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:45.680
pawn, but when it comes to this pawn it's
pretty much powerless. You can't capture because

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:52.680
of rook takes bishop. So how to really maybe
deal with this situation? Black makes this

00:03:54.459 --> 00:04:01.459
c6 move in order to prepare d5. And so instead
of castle in other words in this position,

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:07.569
we have white playing f3 taking this move
into account. Preparing to meet this rook

00:04:07.569 --> 00:04:14.569
to e8 move, which is played, with now not
castle but bishop f2. What is this move doing?

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:21.690
It's getting out of harms way along this e-file
and it's allowing this pawn to have more strength...

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:29.750
it has its strength back. And so this next
move by black, d5, can now be met with e takes

00:04:29.750 --> 00:04:36.750
d and that's exactly what we see. So e takes
d, c takes d, and now c5. So a great change

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:42.940
has just occurred with regard to the pawn
structure. We have a three on two with regard

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:49.130
to the queen side structure. And we also have
an isolated pawn on d5. What that implies

00:04:49.130 --> 00:04:54.919
is that d4 is going to be an important square
for white to maintain control over. And the

00:04:54.919 --> 00:04:59.419
reason for that is because if you don't have
control over it, this pawn can push forward.

00:04:59.419 --> 00:05:05.150
And this is a passed pawn, and you are going
to want to keep it blockaded at its very earliest

00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:11.380
point, and d4 is that point. The other thing
to take note of is that since this pawn is

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:18.320
now on the c5 square, you'll note that its
support points are going to be important for

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:24.919
black to be taking into account. So what are
this pawns support points? Its support points

00:05:24.919 --> 00:05:31.270
are both b6 and d6. b6 really isn't too much
of a concern. It's not like there is going

00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:36.510
to be some knight landing on the b6 square
because we have the a7 pawn watching over

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:43.449
it. But you'll note that the other square,
this d6 square, is very vulnerable to the

00:05:43.449 --> 00:05:48.650
black side right here... it's a hole in the
black position and it has that c5 pawn which

00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:54.470
acts as a supporter for that... a knight let's
say getting to that square. So certainly a

00:05:54.470 --> 00:06:00.600
square to be cautious of is that d6 square.
But before we even get there we have more

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:07.600
development for black, knight c6. Only now
does white castle, and now knight to h5 looking

00:06:07.699 --> 00:06:13.380
to pivot on f4, but white is able to conveniently
defend that with queen to d2. And I use the

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:17.240
word convenient because I think that queen
might have wanted to go there anyway, and

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:22.020
now she is doing a couple things. In fact
she's watching over f4...she is stopping the

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:28.229
knight from pivoting there, and she's also
vacating this d1 square for the queen rook.

00:06:28.229 --> 00:06:34.330
After bishop e5... well what is that move
looking to do? It's lending support to that

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:40.610
f4 square so this move is maybe available,
but white just stops that with g3. And even

00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:46.290
though it weakens the h3 square this really
isn't that big of a problem really for the

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:52.460
white side. The pieces for the black side...
if they can't really crash through, they're

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:59.460
just going to start to become a little bit
misplaced, this guy right here in particular.

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:05.460
One other thing to note is that at the very
early stage in the game, and by very early

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:11.669
I'm referring to move 2... when black makes
this move g6, they are kind of saying that

00:07:11.669 --> 00:07:16.479
one of their minor pieces is going to hold
a greater value than any other minor pieces

00:07:16.479 --> 00:07:20.949
that they have... that piece being their dark
squared bishop. And the reason for that is

00:07:20.949 --> 00:07:27.949
because what does g6 do exactly? Well it prepares
for that bishop to be developed to g7, but

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:34.500
it's also weakening f6 and h6. And so if you
are going to, on the black side, give up your

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:41.500
dark squared bishop in some manner, and if
there are still let's say a lot of pieces

00:07:41.979 --> 00:07:46.199
remaining on the board, in particular the
queens, that could certainly turn out to be

00:07:46.199 --> 00:07:53.199
a problem, and in this game it's a big problem.
So after bishop to e5, we have g3. h3 is weakened.

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:59.759
Black tries to take advantage of that, the
bishop is deep in the white position controlling

00:07:59.759 --> 00:08:05.410
squares near the white king, but you'll note
that even though the rook is attacked, it's

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:10.580
maybe just being chased away to a square it
already wanted to go to. Certainly this file

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:16.840
is going to be a good one for a white rook.
So we have rook e1. After knight to g7 this

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:21.509
is where it starts to maybe show itself a
little bit that the coordination of the black

00:08:21.509 --> 00:08:27.009
pieces is just a little bit off when we have
I guess you'd say a fianchettoed knight. Not

00:08:27.009 --> 00:08:32.240
really the ideal placement for a knight. The
other pieces are maybe well-placed but this

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:37.300
knight right here on g7... not so hot. And
usually it'll just take one poorly placed

00:08:37.300 --> 00:08:41.649
piece in order for your position to maybe
crumble. And the position really does crumble

00:08:41.649 --> 00:08:48.649
very very fast unfortunately for black. Before
we have any really strong move just yet, we

00:08:49.430 --> 00:08:56.430
do have rook on a to d1 getting the last piece
involved, bolstering the center right here.

00:08:56.550 --> 00:09:02.250
These rooks very very well-placed... hard
to suggest better placements for those rooks.

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:09.250
The knight on c3 classically developed, it's
very well on that square. The bishops... it's

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:15.320
a really really nice setup for white. But
again what's really glaring for the black

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:21.850
side unfortunately is that knight on g7. So
rook to c8 trying to get some other piece

00:09:21.850 --> 00:09:27.410
involved. But I think it's just too little
too late at this point unfortunately because

00:09:27.410 --> 00:09:34.020
of the idea... the really simple idea of getting
a knight to that d6 square, and it starts

00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:40.580
with knight on d to b5. And I don't know that
there is really much that black can do in

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:46.560
this position. What black ended up choosing
to do was kick the knight to that square.

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:50.860
And so after bishop takes knight, because
we do have this fork going on, so that knight

00:09:50.860 --> 00:09:56.270
move comes with that much more of an effect.
Not only is it just pivoting on a hole, but

00:09:56.270 --> 00:10:03.200
it's hitting both rooks. So bishop takes knight,
pawn takes bishop, and now d4. This right

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:07.430
here is a pretty interesting position. It
may be very tempting to just to want to go

00:10:07.430 --> 00:10:12.830
ahead and grab that pawn, but white doesn't.
They play knight e4, trying to just focus

00:10:12.830 --> 00:10:18.750
on f6 and that's very telling in this game.
But before we get to that point... if instead

00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:24.769
we did have bishop takes pawn, there's an
interesting variation that I took note of

00:10:24.769 --> 00:10:31.360
in this game and it may have come in the following
form: if knight captures bishop in this position,

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:38.190
queen captures, knight f5 can start to maybe
cause white some problems. And I think black

00:10:38.190 --> 00:10:45.000
could maybe wriggle out of this situation
even though this pawn is very deep and looking

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:50.690
to press forward to d7 to fork both rooks.
What exactly to do in this position? If let's

00:10:50.690 --> 00:10:56.290
say the queen maybe retreats to d2. You'll
note we can have now queen to b6, and this

00:10:56.290 --> 00:11:02.769
is an interesting variation that I ended up
finding. After this check, king h1, knight

00:11:02.769 --> 00:11:09.510
to e3... this can be really problematic for
white. Problematic to the point where black

00:11:09.510 --> 00:11:15.510
could maybe get away with a draw at this point.
The rook is being hit, and if white was maybe

00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:22.140
banking on this particular move working, it
actually doesn't. For example, if d7 in this

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:29.140
position, we could have bishop g2 check, king
g1, knight takes rook discovered check, king

00:11:30.550 --> 00:11:36.880
takes bishop, queen f2 check, king h1, and
after knight takes knight it seems like white

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:41.140
is still going to be quite alright and they
are going to be up a piece after the smoke

00:11:41.140 --> 00:11:46.300
has cleared. But not so fast actually because
after pawn takes knight, we now have rook

00:11:46.300 --> 00:11:53.300
on c to d8. And after pawn takes rook, rook
takes queen, and this queen's position is

00:11:54.810 --> 00:12:00.570
great. She can't be dislodged, she's keeping
the king right in the corner, we have a five

00:12:00.570 --> 00:12:06.230
on five with regard to the pawns, and a situation
here where this bishop is caught in a crossfire...

00:12:06.230 --> 00:12:11.860
pinned in both directions. And the queen with
its pressure on the rook... there is nothing

00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:17.130
that this bishop can do. Even though it's
present on the board it's kind of in a position

00:12:17.130 --> 00:12:22.360
where it just can't move anywhere. And so
there is no progress to be made unfortunately

00:12:22.360 --> 00:12:27.890
in this position. And just showing let's say
one quick draw in this position, it would

00:12:27.890 --> 00:12:32.899
come in the following form: black could always
just make a move like h5, and then move the

00:12:32.899 --> 00:12:38.240
king back and forth on let's say g8 and h7
and there's no progress to be made on the

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:45.160
white side, and it would just turn out to
be a draw. So I don't know that this would

00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:51.050
all be forced... I think it is maybe forced
at this point right here if we did have queen

00:12:51.050 --> 00:12:54.769
to d2, but maybe there is something maybe
a little bit different. But I thought that

00:12:54.769 --> 00:13:01.709
that was one interesting variation which maybe
white had spotted, and just wanted to avoid

00:13:01.709 --> 00:13:08.709
altogether at this point right here. So instead
of in other words capturing on d4, we had

00:13:08.810 --> 00:13:15.810
knight to e4 focusing instead on A) preserving
this bishop, and by doing so you are preventing

00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:21.329
any nonsense that might occur against your
king along this diagonal and B) keeping again

00:13:21.329 --> 00:13:28.329
a close eye on that f6 square. So bishop f5
unfortunately is met with a really nice move,

00:13:29.190 --> 00:13:34.920
and that is d7 landing a fork to these two
rooks. And if queen takes, this is going to

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:41.920
be "lights out" very quick. So instead bishop
takes is necessary and only now at this point

00:13:41.930 --> 00:13:48.930
do we have bishop takes on d4. Because after
knight takes bishop... that is the move that

00:13:49.110 --> 00:13:54.649
gets played but you'll note that getting that
knight to that f6 square is going to be very

00:13:54.649 --> 00:13:59.620
troublesome to the black side After knight
takes bishop, queen takes knight, once again

00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:04.670
this knight is getting ready to pivot on f6.
Again black does not have a dark squared bishop.

00:14:04.670 --> 00:14:09.610
f6, h6... if you could maybe access either
of those squares, look to do that in your

00:14:09.610 --> 00:14:15.360
own games. Certainly f6 is going to be the
one white is looking to get to from such a

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:22.360
position here. There is no way out unfortunately
for black at this point. It's just downhill,

00:14:23.130 --> 00:14:29.010
there is no way to save the game. So how this
actually finishes up is knight to f5. There's

00:14:29.010 --> 00:14:33.970
nothing really else to do....this bishop is
hit twice, the knight is getting ready to

00:14:33.970 --> 00:14:40.970
go to f6. After knight to f5 the queen is
being attacked. White of course just has to

00:14:41.389 --> 00:14:46.750
be maybe a little bit careful here not to
just go forward with knight to f6 check, king

00:14:46.750 --> 00:14:52.480
h8, and this queen is being hit. And she can't
move to a square where she still keeps a close

00:14:52.480 --> 00:14:58.339
eye on f6. And so if the queen now takes on
d7, we would have queen takes knight and black

00:14:58.339 --> 00:15:05.339
is getting out of any trouble. So instead
we just have, of course not just the knight

00:15:05.870 --> 00:15:10.230
going to f6, but just queen takes bishop and
this game finishes up very quickly. Queen

00:15:10.230 --> 00:15:15.649
gives check, king goes to the corner. There's
just a little bit of pressure that black is

00:15:15.649 --> 00:15:21.339
able to place on white, but after white consolidates...
that's going to be very telling. White is

00:15:21.339 --> 00:15:26.730
certainly up a piece at this point, queen
takes pawn. White is up material, so you offer

00:15:26.730 --> 00:15:32.880
queen exchanges. What else is there really
to do? After the exchange of queens, bishop

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:39.880
c4, knight d4 hitting on f3, but after rook
to e3 to defend that pawn... since white has

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:45.980
now consolidated and there's no more pressure
that black can place on white... black ends

00:15:45.980 --> 00:15:50.070
up resigning at this point. So that's all
for this video. As always, I hope you got

00:15:50.070 --> 00:15:51.230
something out of it. Take care, bye.

